## Setup

Use **Delve 1.27.0 or newer**. Verified with 1.27.0 on Go 1.26.

	go install github.com/go-delve/delve/cmd/dlv@latest
	dlv version

Delve enforces a supported Go range and refuses to run outside it:

	Delve 1.26.0 – 1.26.3    supports Go 1.24 – 1.26
	Delve 1.27.0             supports Go 1.25 – 1.27      <- only this one does Go 1.27

Three ways to start a session, all used in this part:

	dlv debug            # compile current package with -N -l and debug it
	dlv exec ./binary    # debug a prebuilt binary
	dlv attach <pid>     # debug an already-running process

: Verify your setup now: cd 03-delve/demo && go build ./... && dlv version
:
: The version pairing matters more than usual this month. Go 1.27 is expected in August 2026, and only Delve 1.27.0 supports it. On a mismatch you get a hard error, not a warning: "Version of Delve is too old for Go version go1.27 (maximum supported version 1.26...)".
:
: --check-go-version=false downgrades it to a warning, but the warning literally says "undefined behavior" — wrong variable values, broken stacks. Upgrade instead.
:
: Also worth telling the room: ./setup.sh only installs Delve when it's missing. It does not check the version of an existing install, so anyone who installed dlv a year ago will sail through setup and fail here. Have them run `dlv version`.

## Keep your variables inspectable

For binaries you build yourself:

	go build -gcflags='all=-N -l' -o program .

`-N` disables optimizations, `-l` disables inlining — which is exactly what
`dlv debug` does for you.

Without these, half your locals are "optimized out" and you'll blame the
debugger.

Watch for `-trimpath` too — release builds often set it, and it strips the
paths Delve needs to find your source.

: Worth 30 seconds. It's the single most common reason someone concludes "Delve doesn't work".
:
: trimpath is the second most common. Delve 1.26.1 and later detect it and warn, rather than silently giving you a session with no source listing.

## The five commands that solve most concurrency bugs

.html figures/five-commands.html

: This is the ten-minute crash course. Drill these five, live at the prompt, before the demo.
:
: Everything else in Delve is a normal debugger. These five are what make it a *Go* debugger.

## Triage by wait reason, before reading any code

`goroutines` output ends in a wait reason:

	[chan send]   [chan receive]   [sync.Mutex.Lock]
	[select]      [sleep]          [IO wait]

Read the reasons first. The distribution tells you the shape of the bug before
you open a single file.

: Habit to instill: don't open the source until the wait reasons have told you where to look. Eight goroutines in sync.Mutex.Lock is a very different bug from eight in chan send.

## 400 goroutines, six lines

.html figures/grouping.html

: This is the single highest-value command for concurrency debugging.
:
: Story in that output: workers wedged on a mutex, so no results; collector starves; workers never return, so wg.Wait never returns, so results never closes. One cause, four symptoms.
:
: A Go sync.Mutex doesn't record its owner — so find the holder the honest way: look at the stacks of the waiters. Seven will look identical and one will be different.

## Filtering and grouping is a query language

	goroutines -with user
	goroutines -with/-without (userloc|curloc|goloc|startloc) <substr>
	goroutines -with/-without running
	goroutines -group (userloc|curloc|goloc|startloc|running|user)
	goroutines -group label <key>
	goroutines -with label key=value
	goroutines -exec <cmd>        # run a command on every match

One syntax gotcha: the expression in `-chan <expr>` must not contain spaces.

If your services set pprof labels via `pprof.Do`, Delve turns them into a
query language for goroutines.

: The label point is the one that lands with people running real services. If you already label by request ID or job type, you can slice a frozen process by those labels.
:
: In the demo: goroutines -group label job shows job=processor/receiver/validator — and no job=shipper. Ask the room what's missing. The shipper is *gone*.

## Context switching

	goroutine 7            # make goroutine 7 current
	goroutine 7 stack      # peek without switching

Once switched, `bt`, `frame 3`, `locals`, and `print` all follow.

: The distinction matters when you're sweeping: `goroutine <id> stack` lets you look at twenty goroutines without losing your place.

## Panics and fatal errors are breakpoints

Delve pre-installs `unrecovered-panic` and `runtime-fatal-throw`.

So this:

	fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!

&hellip; leaves you at a prompt with the corpse still warm.

For hangs the runtime *doesn't* detect: `Ctrl+C` freezes the process at a prompt.

: This is the "oh!" moment for a lot of people. They've seen that fatal error a hundred times and always treated it as the end of the story rather than the beginning.
:
: Exercise 1 is exactly the second case — a silent stall the runtime never detects.

## Two more things worth knowing

**Stepping is goroutine-pinned.** Breakpoints stop the world, but `next` and
`step` stay on *your* goroutine rather than bouncing to whichever goroutine
runs next.

**Breakpoints that do things:**

	condition <bp> <expr>        # only stop when it matters
	on <bp> <command>            # run a command at the stop
	trace <locspec>              # notify, don't stop
	display -a <expr>            # re-print on every stop
	watch [-r|-w|-rw] <expr>     # hardware watchpoint: break on DATA, not code

Before exercise 3: writes that **don't change the value** may not be reported.

: The watchpoint is the setup for exercise 3 — break on data, not code. It's the only way to catch a wrong-value bug where every access is properly locked.
:
: The caveat matters when someone expects a watchpoint to fire on every write that touched the variable. It fires on writes that CHANGED it. If a goroutine writes the same value back, you may not stop.

## Channel forensics

.html figures/hchan.html

: Vocabulary for the room: qcount/dataqsiz = items buffered vs capacity; sendq/recvq = queues of goroutines parked on send/receive (a sudog is the runtime's record of "goroutine waiting on a synchronization object"); closed is 0 or 1.
:
: The buffer isn't opaque — `print jobs.buf` shows real values. In a real system that's how you learn *which* work is stuck, not just how much.
:
: Delve also summarizes the waiters underneath the struct, so you don't have to chase sudog pointers.

## What each piece teaches

- **`demo/`** — a pipeline deadlock, instructor-led: goroutine triage, the
  `-chan` filter, channel internals, root-causing a missing receiver, plus
  conditional breakpoints, tracepoints, `dlv trace`, and `dump`.
- **`ex1-fanout-fanin/`** — a pipeline that stalls *silently*; the runtime
  deadlock detector never fires. Attach or interrupt, group by location, find
  the one goroutine that's different.
- **`ex2-dispatcher/`** — a dispatch/collect deadlock, solved almost entirely by
  reading `print <chan>` output.
- **`ex3-inventory/`** — mutex-protected, race-detector-clean, wrong-*value*.
  A watchpoint catches the culprit mid-write. The most "only a debugger finds
  this" of the three.

: ex2-dispatcher always dies dispatching job 10 — these deadlocks wedge the same way every run. If something behaves oddly, git checkout -- <dir> and rerun.

## Beyond the terminal: debugging a live process

	dlv attach $(pgrep myservice)
	(dlv) goroutines -group userloc

The process is only paused while you sit at the prompt. `quit` asks whether to
kill it — answer **n** to detach and let it keep running.

This is the move when a service is wedged *right now* and a restart would
destroy the evidence.

: On Linux boxes you may need sysctl kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0 or root. On macOS, Delve's installed debugserver handles the signing dance.

## Post-mortem: core dumps

Deadlocked service, can't keep it paused? Freeze-dry it.

	# Option A — from a live Delve session, any platform:
	(dlv) dump ./service.core

	# Option B — on Linux, make fatal errors leave a core behind:
	ulimit -c unlimited
	GOTRACEBACK=crash ./service

	# Either way, debug the corpse later, anywhere with the same binary:
	dlv core ./service ./service.core
	(dlv) goroutines -with user

: dlv core reads Linux ELF cores, Windows minidumps, and Delve dump files. Everything read-only works: goroutines, stacks, print, -chan. You obviously can't continue.
:
: Great for CI: capture cores from hung integration tests and debug them after the fact.

## Scripting Delve

Non-interactive sessions for repeatable investigations — and for agents:

	# run a canned command list
	dlv exec ./service --init deadlock.dlv

	# lightweight function tracing: args, returns, timestamps, no code changes
	dlv trace --timestamp 'SendOrder|Checkout'
	dlv trace -v 3 'SendOrder'          # 0=values 1=types 2=inline 3=expanded 4=full
	dlv trace --follow-calls 2 'Checkout'   # trace callees too

And inside a session, capture everything you do to a file:

	(dlv) transcript session.txt

Delve also embeds Starlark (`source script.star`) for programmatic sessions —
e.g. iterate all goroutines and dump selected frame variables.

: dlv trace is underrated: it's printf debugging without editing or rebuilding the program.
:
: transcript is the concrete answer to "how do I hand this to an AI assistant" — and to "how do I attach evidence to a bug report". -t truncates an existing file, -x suppresses stdout, transcript -off stops it.

## Editors and DAP

`dlv dap` speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol. VS Code's Go extension, GoLand, and
Neovim (nvim-dap) all sit on top of it.

Same engine — so everything in this section exists there too:

- VS Code's **CALL STACK** view is `goroutines`
- conditional breakpoints are a right-click away
- the **DEBUG CONSOLE** accepts `dlv` expressions
- **data breakpoints** (Delve 1.26.2) are watchpoints — exercise 3's technique,
  now a right-click in the Variables pane

Use the GUI day to day if you like. But the CLI is what you have over SSH on the
box where prod is burning — and it's what scripts and agents can drive.

: DAP is where most recent Delve work has landed, and it has closed much of the gap with the CLI: data breakpoints (1.26.2), suspended breakpoints and multi-process follow-exec targets (1.26.0), hit-conditional breakpoints (1.26.1), read (1.26.0) and write (1.27.0) memory handlers, examinemem in the debug console (1.26.2).
:
: If someone in the room lives in VS Code, the data-breakpoint news is the one they'll care about — watchpoints used to be a reason to drop to the CLI.

## The goroutine leak profile

A pprof profile reporting only goroutines the GC can **prove** will never wake up.

	# Go 1.26 — experimental, gated at build time:
	GOEXPERIMENT=goroutineleakprofile go build

	# Go 1.27 — generally available, no flag:
	runtime/pprof.Lookup("goroutineleak")
	/debug/pprof/goroutineleak

The fleet-scale complement to this section: the profile tells you **that**
goroutines leaked and where they're parked. Delve tells you **why**.

: In Go 1.26 this is an experiment gated behind a build-time flag — without the GOEXPERIMENT build the endpoint 404s with "Unknown profile". In Go 1.27 it's generally available and the goroutineleakprofile GOEXPERIMENT setting is deleted outright, so the endpoint is simply there.
:
: Check which one you're on before the demo and say so; the wrong build command is a confusing five minutes.
:
: Caveats that hold in both versions: detection is conservative — it can miss leaks whose primitive stays reachable from a global or from a runnable goroutine's locals, so absence of proof isn't proof of absence. And requesting the profile triggers a full GC cycle that performs the analysis, which is how it stays zero-overhead when unused. Fine on demand or in CI; don't scrape it every second.
:
: Exercise 1's stretch goal runs it against the silent stall, with real captured output.

## AI agents can hold the debugger too

	dlv debug --headless --listen=127.0.0.1:2345 --accept-multiclient
	dlv connect 127.0.0.1:2345    # a human... or not

Headless Delve exposes the full JSON-RPC API; `dlv dap` exposes the DAP one. The
Delve project ships an MCP bridge, `go-delve/mcp-dap-server`, that lets coding
agents set breakpoints and walk goroutine dumps through those same protocols.

An agent that can walk a frozen process reasons from evidence. An agent that
can't is pattern-matching on your source.

Either way, *someone* has to know what a `sendq` full of parked goroutines
means. That's you.

: That last line is the closing note of the whole workshop. Land it.
:
: The finale (ex4-agent) wires this into Claude Code and lets an agent debug exercise 2's deadlock in front of the room: launch, goroutine sweep, frame-targeted evaluation, written verdict.
:
: Failure modes are scripted in the ex4-agent README — server needs dlv on PATH; tools appear only after a session starts; evaluate needs a frame at the fatal-error stop. If the agent flails, the fallback is doing the same investigation by hand, which is itself the discussion point.

## Wrap-up: when to reach for Delve

- **When the program is stuck right now** and you need to know why, not guess.
- **When a restart would destroy the evidence** — `dlv attach`, or `dump` a core.
- **When the value is wrong but every access is locked** — watchpoints.
- **When you're debugging code you didn't write** — triage 400 goroutines
  without reading 400 goroutines.

What it *can't* tell you: whether an access was racy without a race (Part I),
or where the time went across a whole run (Part II).

Three tools. Three kinds of ground truth. Use all three.

: Close the arc explicitly — this is the last section, so the callback to Parts I and II is the whole point.
:
: Then: what to run in CI tomorrow. -race in CI, flight recorder in prod, dlv attach in your pocket.

